libxml2 for OpenVMS
===================

This is libxml2 @VERSION@ built natively for OpenVMS @ARCH@ with VSI clang
(LP64), from the unmodified libxml2 release plus the VMS files in the
vms-libxml2 project:

    https://github.com/issinoho/vms-libxml2

Kit version: @KITVERSION@.  libxml2 is distributed under the MIT licence;
see COPYRIGHT.  The API is documented at https://gnome.pages.gitlab.gnome.org/libxml2/
and in the headers themselves.

What is installed
-----------------
    LIBXML2$ROOT:[INCLUDE.LIBXML]*.H     the libxml2 API (<libxml/parser.h> ...)
    LIBXML2$ROOT:[LIB]LIBXML2.OLB        the library (object library)
    LIBXML2$ROOT:[BIN]XMLLINT.EXE        libxml2's command-line tool
    LIBXML2$ROOT:[DOC]                   this file, COPYRIGHT, NEWS., README.MD

Features: tree, SAX1/2, push parser, reader, writer, output, HTML, XPath,
XPointer, XInclude, C14N, catalogs, DTD validation, regular expressions,
RELAX NG and XML Schemas.  Not built: threads, iconv (libxml2's built-in
UTF-8/16 and ISO-8859-x tables are used), ICU, zlib, HTTP, dynamic modules,
Schematron.

Setting up
----------
The kit installs LIBXML2$STARTUP.COM into SYS$STARTUP and runs it once.  To
define LIBXML2$ROOT at every boot, the system manager adds this line to
SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:

    $ @SYS$STARTUP:LIBXML2$STARTUP.COM

Building a program against libxml2
----------------------------------
The library is built with VSI clang (64-bit long and pointers): link it only
into programs compiled with clang, never with VSI C (32-bit long and
pointers).  Use the same name shortening as the library:

    $ clang -names2=shortened -I/libxml2$root/include -c myprog.c
    $ LINK myprog, LIBXML2$ROOT:[LIB]LIBXML2.OLB/LIBRARY

The library was built with -fno-builtin-memset -fno-builtin-bzero: VSI clang
lowers memset() and bzero() to OTS$FILL but assumes memset's return value,
which miscompiles "memset(p, 0, n); return p;".  Use the same flags in your
own clang code.

xmllint
-------
    $ xmllint :== $LIBXML2$ROOT:[BIN]XMLLINT.EXE
    $ xmllint "--noout" "--valid" doc.xml
    $ xmllint "--xpath" "count(//item)" doc.xml

Quote arguments (and SET PROCESS/PARSE_STYLE=EXTENDED) to keep their case.
xmllint reads files byte by byte: use Stream_LF files.
